{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 19, :comments ["<b>The stork (חסידה).</b> Tsigunia [in Old French]. It does kindness (חסידות) with its fellows, as Rashi explained." "<b>The heron (אנפה).</b> The heron (יורון) in la'az. It becomes angry quickly (תתאנף)." "<b>The hoopoe (דוכיפת).</b> The hoopoe (רופא), its feet are flat (כפות), as Rashi explained." "<b>The bat (עטלף).</b> Gurot [in Old French]. A small creature that flits and flies at night and has no eyes, as we say commonly — a parable of a rooster and a bat that were waiting for the light."], :book :leviticus, :voice :chizkuni, :lang :en, :chapter 11, :notes {2 "Rashi explains that דוכיפת is a bird whose toes are joined together; the Old French term hupé is supplied."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T15:11:22.008792173Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}